    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access — Hardware Additions", "technique": "T1200", "what_happened": "At 2026-03-12 01:11:18 UTC, Suricata / Wazuh logged a new-host event on the 10.108.57.0/24 corporate VLAN: a previously-unseen device claiming IP 10.108.57.177 with source MAC b8:27:eb:a4:7c:19. The MAC OUI prefix b8:27:eb is the registered Raspberry Pi Foundation block — a single-board computer is NOT a sanctioned corporate asset class on this production subnet. The device appeared at 01:11 UTC, outside business hours, with no preceding DHCP lease request matching a known asset-inventory entry. Wazuh rule 92810 'Unknown MAC address detected on corp VLAN' fired with MITRE T1200.", "chain_reason": "Suricata new-host / DHCP signature plus Wazuh rule 92810 fired with data.srcip=10.108.57.177 and rule.description text containing the MAC b8:27:eb:a4:7c:19. The univariate signal: a data.srcip on the corporate /24 that had zero prior alert history and a MAC OUI not present in the approved-vendor list.", "first_seen": "2026-03-12T01:11:18+0000"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access — Rogue Device Fingerprint", "technique": "T1200", "what_happened": "Within seconds of the new-host event, Wazuh rule 92811 'Rogue device fingerprint: Raspberry Pi / IoT OUI' fired, correlating the MAC OUI b8:27:eb against the known-rogue-OUI watchlist (Raspberry Pi b8:27:eb / dc:a6:32 / e4:5f:01, TP-Link 50:c7:bf, D-Link 14:91:82, cheap-IoT 00:0d:b9). The device fingerprint — single-board computer OUI, off-hours appearance, corporate VLAN, not in CMDB — matches the classic drop-box / implant profile: an attacker who gained brief physical access plugged a small concealable computer into a live wall port or switch.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92811 fired with rule.mitre.id=T1200 and rule.description containing 'Raspberry Pi' and the OUI b8:27:eb. The rule correlates step-1's unknown MAC against the rogue-OUI watchlist — escalating from 'unknown device' (rule 92810) to 'fingerprinted rogue hardware class' (rule 92811).", "first_seen": "2026-03-12T01:11:24+0000"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Discovery — Internal ARP Reconnaissance", "technique": "T1018", "what_happened": "Beginning 01:14:30 UTC, the rogue device (data.srcip=10.108.57.177) generated a burst of ARP-level probe traffic across the 10.108.57.0/24 subnet — sweeping the gateway 10.108.57.1 and internal hosts including 10.108.57.10 and 10.108.57.50. Suricata fired ARP-scan / 'ET SCAN' style signatures. This is the canonical post-drop behaviour of a network implant: enumerate the live-host inventory of the segment it landed on before attempting any service interaction or callback.", "chain_reason": "Suricata ARP-scan signatures with data.srcip=10.108.57.177 against multiple data.dstip values in 10.108.57.0/24 within a short window. The rogue device pivoting from passive presence (steps 1-2) to active internal discovery (step 3) is the implant-activation signature.", "first_seen": "2026-03-12T01:14:30+0000"},
